# FileMate Capstone: Sample-by-Sample Build Guide

Stack: React (Vite) + FastAPI + plain OpenAI tool-calling loop. 
Purpose: build the capstone as 13 small working samples (S0 to S12). Each sample is runnable and verifiable before the next starts. A master verification prompt audits the whole assignment at the end.

---

## How to use this guide

1. Open a fresh agent session on the build machine, in an empty folder named `filemate`.
2. Paste the **Shared Context** block once at the start of the session. Every sample prompt assumes it.
3. For each sample, in order:
   1. Read **Expected** and **Learn**, so you know what you are building and why.
   2. Paste the **Build prompt**.
   3. Paste the **Verify prompt** (best in a fresh session or sub-agent, so the reviewer is independent of the builder).
   4. Move on only when verification is all PASS. If something fails, paste the failure table back to the builder and say "fix only these".
4. After S12, paste the **Master Verification Prompt**.
5. No Git or any other version control is used, anywhere. To be able to roll back, copy the whole `filemate` folder after each green sample (for example `filemate_backup_S3`, kept OUTSIDE the project folder, without `.env`, `node_modules` and `.venv`). If a later sample breaks something, restore from the last good copy.

There is no time limit. The assignment's 3-hour budget is only a guide, so take each sample at your own pace and do not move on until its verification is all PASS.

If you ever decide to cut scope, S6 (the approval gate) is the most valuable sample, then S3 (validators), then S10 (logging). Record anything you skipped under Known Gaps in the README.

Before starting:
- Have your OpenAI API key ready. It goes only in the local `.env`, never in any other file.
- Nothing is provided for the mock Expense API (no stub), so S2 builds it from scratch, in memory, from the contract in the Shared Context.
- The 60-day rule is in the assignment ("Receipt date within the last 60 days and not in the future"), and the guide uses the real current date for it. The sample receipts are dated 28-30 Oct 2026, so they stay valid until 27 Dec 2026 (60 days after 28 Oct). After that date, R1-R4 and the other fixed-date tests fail with DATE_TOO_OLD. That is correct behaviour, so for later runs just re-date the test receipts to within the last 60 days.

